        {
          "id": "def-qftx-adhm-linear-data-stability-and-regularity",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "library": "mathematics",
          "title": "ADHM linear data, stability, costability and FI levels",
          "reference": "items/def-qftx-adhm-linear-data-stability-and-regularity.md#Definition",
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_roles": {},
          "statement": "Fix integers $N\\ge1$ and $k\\ge0$ and standard Hermitian spaces $V=\\mathbb C^k$, $W=\\mathbb C^N$. A framed ADHM linear datum is $x=(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ with $B_i\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$, $I:W\\to V$ and $J:V\\to W$. Define the complex and real polynomial expressions\n\n$$\\mu_{\\mathbb C}(x)=[B_1,B_2]+IJ,\\qquad \\mu_{\\mathbb R}(x)=[B_1,B_1^\\dagger]+[B_2,B_2^\\dagger]+II^\\dagger-J^\\dagger J.$$\n\nThe datum is stable if the vectors $B_{i_1}\\cdots B_{i_m}I(w)$, with $m\\ge0$, $i_j\\in\\{1,2\\}$ and $w\\in W$, span $V$. It is costable if there is no nonzero subspace of $V$ invariant under both $B_i$ and contained in $\\ker J$. It is regular if it is stable and costable. The group $GL(V)$ acts by $g\\cdot x=(gB_1g^{-1},gB_2g^{-1},gI,Jg^{-1})$. Its unitary subgroup is $U(V)$.\n\nWrite $X^s=\\{x:\\mu_{\\mathbb C}(x)=0,\\ x\\text{ stable}\\}$, $X^{\\mathrm{reg}}=\\{x\\in X^s:x\\text{ costable}\\}$ and, for real $\\zeta\\ge0$, $X_\\zeta^U=\\{x\\in X^s:\\mu_{\\mathbb R}(x)=\\zeta I_V\\}$. Denote the corresponding orbit sets by $X^s/GL(V)$, $X^{\\mathrm{reg}}/GL(V)$ and $X_\\zeta^U/U(V)$, with quotient topologies when discussing their analytic spaces. A word frame consists of $k$ columns obtained by fixed words in $B_1,B_2$ applied to fixed standard basis vectors of $W$.\n\nFor $T_{\\mathbb C}=(\\mathbb C^*)^2\\times(\\mathbb C^*)^N$, write $t_1,t_2,e_1,\\ldots,e_N$ for the coordinate characters and put $h=\\operatorname{diag}(e_1^{-1},\\ldots,e_N^{-1})$. Its action is $(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot x=(t_1B_1,t_2B_2,Ih^{-1},t_1t_2hJ)$. The compact torus is its subgroup on which all characters have modulus one. The framing representation thus has character $\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha^{-1}$ and its dual has character $\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha$.\n\nThese are finite-dimensional matrix definitions. They do not assert a real/complex quotient correspondence, a sheaf or ASD identification, or a noncompact pushforward. In particular, positive-FI stability and zero-FI regularity are distinct conditions; neither an endpoint identification nor an ordinary ASD interpretation follows from this definition.",
          "strategy": "",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
