items/lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus.md:70:1.2 Localize by the fixed finite partition and extend each compactly supported coefficient by zero into a bounded Euclidean ball. For $p=2$, $2k>d$; [F2] makes each localized $H^k$ coefficient a Banach algebra and gives its $C^1$ representative when $k>d/2+1$. The finite sum of these estimates proves the same algebra and embedding bounds for sections, matrix-valued functions, and forms in the chosen bundle atlas. [F1, F2, step 1.1]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus.md:72:2.1 The finite-dimensional matrix exponential is smooth, has derivative the identity at zero on $\mathfrak{su}(N)$, and maps $\mathfrak{su}(N)$ into $SU(N)$. In the Banach algebra from step 1.2 its power series and every termwise derivative converge in $H^{k+1}$ on a sufficiently small ball, since the algebra norm is submultiplicative after an equivalent rescaling. The matrix logarithm series converges on a neighborhood of the identity; for a unitary matrix sufficiently close to identity its logarithm is anti-Hermitian, and its trace is zero because the determinant is one and the sum of the small eigenvalue angles lies in $2\pi\mathbb Z$. Hence pointwise $\exp$ and $\log$ give inverse smooth maps between neighborhoods of zero in $H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)$ and the identity in $\mathcal G_{k+1}$. The inverse-function theorem [F3] also supplies these local Banach charts. Their translated charts cover the group; changes of chart are smooth because the transition functions of $\operatorname{Ad}P$ act by smooth conjugation and the multiplication is a bounded bilinear Sobolev map. The pointwise product and inverse therefore close on $\mathcal G_{k+1}$ and are smooth, giving the asserted Hilbert Lie group. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.2]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus.md:74:3.1 In a local trivialization the action is $A^u=u^{-1}Au+u^{-1}du$. Step 1.2 bounds multiplication $H^{k+1}\times H^k\to H^k$ because $H^{k+1}\hookrightarrow H^k$ continuously and $H^k$ is an algebra. Thus the first term is smooth in $(A,u)$. In the charts of step 2.1, inversion is smooth and differentiation $H^{k+1}\to H^k$ is bounded. The derivative of the second term $f(u)=u^{-1}du$ in a tangent direction $\eta$ is $Df(u)[\eta]=-u^{-1}\eta u^{-1}du+u^{-1}d\eta$. Every further derivative is a finite sum of products of $u^{-1}$, tangent factors $\eta_i$, and at most one differentiated tangent factor $d\eta_j$; each product is bounded in $H^k$ by step 1.2. Finite-dimensional Taylor remainders for matrix inversion, multiplied using the same algebra estimate, tend to zero in the required Sobolev norm at every order. Therefore $f$ and the full gauge action are smooth. [F1, step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus.md:76:4.1 For $t\in\mathbb R$, expand in the finite-dimensional matrix algebra: $e^{-t\xi}(A+a)e^{t\xi}=A+a+t[A,\xi]+o(t)$ in $H^k$, while $e^{-t\xi}d(e^{t\xi})=t\,d\xi+o(t)$ in $H^k$ by the bounded derivative and product estimates of step 1.2. Adding the two terms gives derivative $a+d\xi+[A,\xi]=a+d_A\xi$, which is the displayed formula. [F1, step 1.2, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:102:1.2 The tangent space of the Coulomb slice at $A$ is $\ker d_A^*$. By [F3], the bounded linear map $L_A:\ker d_A^*\oplus H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)\to H^k(T^*M\otimes\operatorname{ad}P)$, $L_A(a,\xi)=a+d_A\xi$, is an isomorphism with inverse $v\mapsto(v-d_AG_Ad_A^*v,G_Ad_A^*v)$. The derivative of $(a,\xi)\mapsto(A+a)^{\exp\xi}$ at $(0,0)$ is $L_A$ by [F1]. [F1, F3]
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:106:2.2 Apply the inverse-function theorem [F4] to $\Phi_A(s,u):=s^u$ on the local Banach product $S_{A,\varepsilon}\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ near $(A,e)$; its derivative is $L_A$ from step 1.2. Shrink $\varepsilon$ so the slice remains in the open irreducible locus. At $(s,e)$, for $s=A+a$, the derivative is $L_s(b,\xi)=b+d_s\xi$ on $b\in\ker d_A^*$; this differs from $L_A$ only by $(b,\xi)\mapsto[a,\xi]$. Its operator norm is at most $C\|a\|_{H^k}$ by [F7]. Choose $\varepsilon$ so $C\varepsilon\|L_A^{-1}\|<1$; then [F5] makes $L_s$ an isomorphism for every $s$ in the slice. At an arbitrary $(s,u_0)$, write $u=u_0v$ near $u_0$ and use $s^{u_0v}=(s^{u_0})^v$. Applying the inverse action by $u_0$ to the target tangent and changing the gauge tangent by the invertible adjoint action identifies this derivative with $L_s$. Hence the derivative is an isomorphism at every point of the slice product, and the inverse-function theorem makes $\Phi_A$ a local diffeomorphism throughout. [F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, step 1.2]
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:108:3.1 The map $\Phi_A$ is injective after possibly shrinking the slice further. Otherwise there would be sequences $s_j,t_j\in S_{A,1/j}$ and distinct pairs $(s_j,u_j)\ne(t_j,v_j)$ with $s_j^{u_j}=t_j^{v_j}$. Put $w_j=u_jv_j^{-1}$; then $s_j^{w_j}=t_j$ and $s_j,t_j\to A$. Properness [F2] gives a subsequence of $w_j$ converging to $w$ with $A^w=A$. Freeness [F2] gives $w=e$. Hence for all sufficiently large indices, both $(s_j,w_j)$ and $(t_j,e)$ lie in the local injectivity neighborhood from step 2.2. Their equal images force $s_j=t_j$ and $w_j=e$, and freeness then gives $u_j=v_j$, contradicting distinctness. [F2, step 2.2]
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:110:3.2 Let $U_A:=\Phi_A(S_{A,\varepsilon}\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1})$. It is open because $\Phi_A$ is a local diffeomorphism at every point of its domain, and it is exactly the saturation of the slice. By steps 3.1 and 2.2, $\Phi_A$ is a bijective local diffeomorphism onto $U_A$, hence a diffeomorphism. If $V_A:=\pi_k(S_{A,\varepsilon})$, then $\pi_k^{-1}(V_A)=U_A$. For every open subset $O$ of the slice, $O\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ is open and its image under $\Phi_A$ is open; thus $\pi_k(O)$ is open in $V_A$ by the quotient topology. Therefore the slice-to-quotient map is a smooth chart and $\Phi_A$ is an equivariant local principal trivialization. [step 2.2, step 3.1, algebra]
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:112:4.1 The orbit relation on $\mathcal A_k^*$ is closed: if $x_j^{u_j}=y_j$ and $(x_j,y_j)\to(x,y)$, properness [F2] gives a convergent subsequence $u_{j_\ell}\to u$, and continuity of the action gives $x^u=y$. For distinct orbits, the complement of this closed relation contains their pair, so there are product neighborhoods $O_x\times O_y$ disjoint from the relation. Their saturations are open and disjoint: if the saturations intersected, some translate of a point in $O_x$ would equal a translate of a point in $O_y$, making that pair related. Their quotient images therefore separate the two orbits. This proves the quotient is Hausdorff. [F1, F2, step 3.2]

