  | [F1] The configuration definition fixes the finite-atlas norms, $\operatorname{ad}P$, the right-action formula, and the covariant derivative $d_A\xi=d\xi+[A,\xi]$. [[def-qftx-smooth-sobolev-gauge-configuration]]
  | 
  | [F2] On a bounded extension domain, $W^{k,2}$ is a Banach algebra when $2k>d$, and the higher-order embedding gives $H^k\hookrightarrow C^1$ when $k>d/2+1$. The same estimates apply componentwise to finitely many localized bundle coefficients. [[cor-sobolev-algebra-above-the-critical-index]], [[thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding]]
  | 
  | [F3] A $C^1$ map between real Banach spaces whose derivative at a point is a bounded linear isomorphism is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism between suitable open neighborhoods. [[thm-inverse-function-theorem-for-banach-spaces]]
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | 1.1 On an overlap of two relatively compact bundle charts, a localized section changes coordinates by multiplication by a smooth transition matrix and by a smooth coordinate change. Every derivative of order at most $k$ of the transformed coefficient is a finite sum of bounded derivatives of these transition maps times derivatives of the original coefficient of order at most $k$. The Jacobians and inverse Jacobians are bounded on the compact supports. The product and chain rules therefore bound each localized $H^k$ norm by a constant times the finite-atlas norm in the other chart. Summing over the finite cover gives both norm inequalities. Thus the norms are equivalent and define the same complete Sobolev topology. [given, A1, F1, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 Localize by the fixed finite partition and extend each compactly supported coefficient by zero into a bounded Euclidean ball. For $p=2$, $2k>d$; [F2] makes each localized $H^k$ coefficient a Banach algebra and gives its $C^1$ representative when $k>d/2+1$. The finite sum of these estimates proves the same algebra and embedding bounds for sections, matrix-valued functions, and forms in the chosen bundle atlas. [F1, F2, step 1.1]
  | 
  | 3.1 The finite-dimensional matrix exponential is smooth, has derivative the identity at zero on $\mathfrak{su}(N)$, and maps $\mathfrak{su}(N)$ into $SU(N)$. In the Banach algebra from step 2.1 its power series and every termwise derivative converge in $H^{k+1}$ on a sufficiently small ball, since the algebra norm is submultiplicative after an equivalent rescaling. The matrix logarithm series converges on a neighborhood of the identity; for a unitary matrix sufficiently close to identity its logarithm is anti-Hermitian, and its trace is zero because the determinant is one and the sum of the small eigenvalue angles lies in $2\pi\mathbb Z$. Hence pointwise $\exp$ and $\log$ give inverse smooth maps between neighborhoods of zero in $H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)$ and the identity in $\mathcal G_{k+1}$. The inverse-function theorem [F3] also supplies these local Banach charts. Their translated charts cover the group; changes of chart are smooth because the transition functions of $\operatorname{Ad}P$ act by smooth conjugation and the multiplication is a bounded bilinear Sobolev map. The pointwise product and inverse therefore close on $\mathcal G_{k+1}$ and are smooth, giving the asserted Hilbert Lie group. [F1, F2, F3, step 2.1]
  | 
  | 4.1 In a local trivialization the action is $A^u=u^{-1}Au+u^{-1}du$. Step 2.1 bounds multiplication $H^{k+1}\times H^k\to H^k$ because $H^{k+1}\hookrightarrow H^k$ continuously and $H^k$ is an algebra. Thus the first term is smooth in $(A,u)$. In the charts of step 3.1, inversion is smooth and differentiation $H^{k+1}\to H^k$ is bounded. The derivative of the second term $f(u)=u^{-1}du$ in a tangent direction $\eta$ is $Df(u)[\eta]=-u^{-1}\eta u^{-1}du+u^{-1}d\eta$. Every further derivative is a finite sum of products of $u^{-1}$, tangent factors $\eta_i$, and at most one differentiated tangent factor $d\eta_j$; each product is bounded in $H^k$ by step 2.1. Finite-dimensional Taylor remainders for matrix inversion, multiplied using the same algebra estimate, tend to zero in the required Sobolev norm at every order. Therefore $f$ and the full gauge action are smooth. [F1, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 5.1 For $t\in\mathbb R$, expand in the finite-dimensional matrix algebra: $e^{-t\xi}(A+a)e^{t\xi}=A+a+t[A,\xi]+o(t)$ in $H^k$, while $e^{-t\xi}d(e^{t\xi})=t\,d\xi+o(t)$ in $H^k$ by the bounded derivative and product estimates of step 2.1. Adding the two terms gives derivative $a+d\xi+[A,\xi]=a+d_A\xi$, which is the displayed formula. [F1, step 2.1, step 4.1, algebra] ∎
REPAIR items/lem-qftx-gauge-stabilizer-and-properness.md: passes only after auto-repair — adopt the canonical form:
  | **Given:** Full AC; the compact connected Riemannian bundle data; $k>d/2+1$; and the right gauge action fixed in the preceding definition.
  | 
  | [A1] The Axiom of Choice is the inherited axiom base. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]
  | 
  | [F1] The spaces, finite-atlas norms, right-action formula, and irreducibility convention are those of the preceding definition; $H^k$ embeds continuously in $C^1$. [[def-qftx-smooth-sobolev-gauge-configuration]], [[lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus]]
  | 
  | [F2] The parallel-transport ODE has a common-interval continuous-dependence estimate for nearby coefficients and parameters. A piecewise smooth loop is handled by finitely many such intervals. [[thm-continuous-dependence-of-odes-on-initial-data-and-parameters]]
  | 
  | [F3] Every bounded equicontinuous sequence of curves from a compact interval into finite-dimensional Euclidean space has a uniformly convergent subsequence. [[lem-equicontinuous-bounded-rn-valued-curves-have-a-uniformly-convergent-subsequence]]
  | 
  | [F4] Every bounded sequence in $H^{k+1}$ of sections on the compact manifold has a subsequence converging in $H^k$. [[thm-sobolev-compactness-and-elliptic-estimates-give-fredholm-hodge-decomposition]]
  | 
  | [F5] A bounded operator intertwining an irreducible complex unitary representation with itself is scalar. [[thm-schurs-lemma-for-unitary-representations]]
  | 
  | [F6] The finite-atlas Sobolev product bounds hold in every integer order $0\le r\le k$: $\|fg\|_{H^r}\le C\|f\|_{H^k}\|g\|_{H^r}$. They follow by the product rule, the embeddings in [F1], and Hölder; when derivative orders are $b$ and $c$ with $b+c\le r$, the residual Sobolev orders $k-b$ and $r-c$ have sum at least $k>d/2$, so exponents can be chosen with reciprocal sum at most $1/2$. [[thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding]], [[cor-sobolev-algebra-above-the-critical-index]], [[lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus]]
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Given:** The hypotheses of the Statement.
  | 
  | 1.1 A gauge section $u$ stabilizes $A$ exactly when the local action formula gives $du=uA-Au$, that is, $d_Au=0$. This equation says that $u$ is parallel for the induced connection on $\operatorname{Ad}P$. Since $M$ is connected, parallel transport determines $u$ everywhere from its value at $p_m$, so evaluation is injective on the stabilizer. A parallel section's value commutes with transport around every based loop, hence lies in the holonomy centralizer. [given, A1, F1, algebra]
  | 
  | 1.2 Let $B_j=A_j^{u_j}$ and work in the fixed finite atlas. The local action formula gives $du_j=u_jB_j-A_ju_j$. Since $u_j$ is unitary, its pointwise matrix norm is bounded uniformly. The convergent sequences $A_j,B_j$ are bounded in $H^k$, so the equation gives a uniform $H^1$ bound on $u_j$. If a uniform $H^r$ bound holds for some $0\le r\le k$, [F6] bounds both products on the right in $H^r$; hence $du_j$ is bounded in $H^r$ and $u_j$ is bounded in $H^{r+1}$. Induction through $r=k$ gives a uniform $H^{k+1}$ bound. [given, F1, F6, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 Conversely, let $g\in SU(N)$ commute with every element of $\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A)$. Transport $g$ from $p_m$ along any path to a point $x$ and conjugate it by the path's parallel-transport matrix. Two paths give the same value because their concatenation has holonomy commuting with $g$. In local coordinates this gives a parallel section $u$ with $u(p_m)=g$. It is $C^1$ since $A\in H^k\hookrightarrow C^1$ and the parameter-dependent parallel-transport ODE is $C^1$ in the endpoint. The equation $du=uA-Au$, the bounds [F6], and induction on $r=0,1,\ldots,k$ show $u\in H^{k+1}$: starting with its $C^1$ local coefficients, if $u\in H^r$ then the right side is in $H^r$, so all first derivatives are in $H^r$ and $u\in H^{r+1}$. Thus $u\in\mathcal G_{k+1}$ and stabilizes $A$, proving that evaluation has exactly the stated image. [given, F1, F2, F6, step 1.1]
  | 
  | 2.2 By [F4], a subsequence converges strongly in $H^k$ to a section $u$. The $H^k\hookrightarrow C^0$ embedding preserves the pointwise $SU(N)$ equations and the bundle transition law, so $u$ is a group-bundle section. In the gauge equation, $A_j\to A$, $B_j\to B$, and $u_j\to u$ in $H^k$; because $H^k$ is an algebra, $u_jB_j-A_ju_j\to uB-Au$ in $H^k$. Thus $du_j$ converges in $H^k$ and the local coefficients converge in $H^{k+1}$. The limit satisfies $du=uB-Au$, equivalently $B=A^u$, and $u\in\mathcal G_{k+1}$. [F1, F2, F4, step 1.2]
  | 
  | 3.1 On the standard $\mathbb C^N$, the holonomy matrices are unitary. If the representation is irreducible, [F5] makes every matrix commuting with the representation a scalar $\lambda I_N$. Membership in $SU(N)$ gives $|\lambda|=1$ and $\lambda^N=1$, hence its centralizer is $Z_N$. The stabilizer result of steps 1.1–1.2 therefore gives full stabilizer $Z_N$ and trivial reduced stabilizer. [F5, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 4.1 For each $1\le r<N$, represent an $r$-plane in $\mathbb C^N$ by its Hermitian orthogonal projection $Q$, so the Grassmannian is $\{Q:Q^*=Q,\ Q^2=Q,\ \operatorname{tr}Q=r\}$ in the finite-dimensional matrix space. Such projections have norm one. Regard any sequence of them as constant curves on $[0,1]$; [F3] gives a uniformly convergent subsequence, and the three defining equations pass to its limit. Thus each Grassmannian is compact. Suppose $A_j\to A$ and each $A_j$ is reducible. A subsequence has a fixed invariant dimension $r$, and by compactness another subsequence of invariant projections converges to $Q$. For every fixed based loop $\gamma$, [F2] and [F1] give $\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A_j,\gamma)\to\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A,\gamma)$. Since the holonomy matrices are unitary and preserve $\operatorname{ran}Q_j$, they commute with $Q_j$; passing to the limit shows that $\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A,\gamma)$ commutes with $Q$ for every loop. Therefore $\operatorname{ran}Q$ is a nonzero proper invariant subspace and $A$ is reducible. The reducible locus is closed, so $\mathcal A_k^*$ is open. [F1, F2, F3, step 3.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 5.1 The action map is continuous by the preceding Sobolev-calculus lemma. For a compact subset of $\mathcal A_k^*\times\mathcal A_k^*$, any sequence in its inverse image has a subsequence whose connection and transformed-connection coordinates converge in that compact set. Steps 1.2–3.2 then give a further subsequence of gauge classes converging in $\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$. The inverse image is closed because the action map is continuous, so sequential compactness in these metrizable spaces is compactness. This proves properness. By step 3.1 the reduced action is free. [F1, step 3.1, step 1.2, step 2.2] ∎
REPAIR items/lem-qftx-covariant-laplacian-sobolev-splitting.md: passes only after auto-repair — adopt the canonical form:
  | **Given:** Full AC; the compact Riemannian bundle data; an irreducible $A\in\mathcal A_k^*$; and the integer $k>d/2+1$.
  | 
  | [A1] Full AC is retained through the elliptic, Hodge, and Fredholm suppliers. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]
  | 
  | [F1] The covariant derivative and formal adjoint have the local coefficient formulas fixed in the configuration definition. [[def-qftx-smooth-sobolev-gauge-configuration]]
  | 
  | [F2] For irreducible $A$, the holonomy centralizer is $Z_N$, hence the only covariantly constant adjoint section is zero. [[lem-qftx-gauge-stabilizer-and-properness]]
  | 
  | [F3] For a smooth connection $A_0$, $\Delta_{A_0}$ is a smooth-coefficient, formally symmetric, nonnegative Laplace-type operator; the compact Hodge theorem gives a finite-dimensional smooth kernel, closed range, and the decomposition $H^r=\ker\Delta_{A_0}\oplus\Delta_{A_0}(H^{r+2})$ for every integer $r\ge0$. [[thm-sobolev-compactness-and-elliptic-estimates-give-fredholm-hodge-decomposition]], [[thm-elliptic-differential-operators-on-compact-vector-bundles-have-parametrices-and-regularity]]
  | 
  | [F4] The compact Sobolev inclusions $H^{s+1}\hookrightarrow H^s$ hold for all integer $s\ge0$, and the smooth reference operator has the elliptic estimate $\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}\le C(\|\Delta_{A_0}\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}+\|\xi\|_{L^2})$. [[thm-sobolev-compactness-and-elliptic-estimates-give-fredholm-hodge-decomposition]], [[thm-elliptic-differential-operators-on-compact-vector-bundles-have-parametrices-and-regularity]]
  | 
  | [F5] The interpolation estimate $\|u\|_{H^k}\le\varepsilon\|u\|_{H^{k+1}}+C_\varepsilon\|u\|_{L^2}$ on compact manifolds follows by finite-atlas localization from the stated lower-order Sobolev interpolation inequality. [[lem-interpolation-absorbs-lower-order-sobolev-terms-in-elliptic-estimates]]
  | 
  | [F6] A compact perturbation of a Fredholm operator is Fredholm with the same index. [[thm-fredholm-index-is-stable-under-compact-perturbations]]
  | 
  | [F7] If $T:X\to Y$ is a bounded isomorphism of Banach spaces and $E$ is bounded with $\|T^{-1}E\|<1$, then $T+E$ is invertible by the Neumann series. [[lem-neumann-series-and-small-perturbations-of-bounded-inverses]]
  | 
  | [F8] For $k>d/2+1$, $H^k$ is a Banach algebra and the finite-atlas multiplier estimates needed below follow from the Sobolev embeddings and Hölder. [[cor-sobolev-algebra-above-the-critical-index]], [[thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding]], [[lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus]]
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Given:** The hypotheses of the Statement.
  | 
  | 1.1 Fix the smooth reference connection $A_0$ from the configuration data and set $h=A-A_0\in H^k(T^*M\otimes\operatorname{ad}P)$. In a local orthonormal frame, $d_A=d_{A_0}+[h,\cdot]$ on zero-forms and $d_A^*=d_{A_0}^*-\iota_h$ on one-forms, where $\iota_h(a)=g^{ij}[h_i,a_j]$. Therefore $K_A:=\Delta_A-\Delta_{A_0}$ is a finite sum of terms of the forms $(\nabla_{A_0}h)\xi$, $h\nabla_{A_0}\xi$, and $h^2\xi$. These terms define a bounded map $H^{k+1}\to H^{k-1}$. They are compact: $(\nabla_{A_0}h)\xi$ factors through the compact inclusion $H^{k+1}\hookrightarrow H^{k-1}$ followed by multiplication in the algebra $H^{k-1}$; $h\nabla_{A_0}\xi$ and $h^2\xi$ first map boundedly into $H^k$ and then use the compact inclusion $H^k\hookrightarrow H^{k-1}$. Here $k-1>d/2$, so the needed multiplication bounds follow from [F8]. [given, A1, F4, F8, algebra]
  | 
  | 2.1 Apply [F3] with $r=k-1\ge0$ to $\Delta_{A_0}:H^{k+1}\to H^{k-1}$. Its kernel is finite dimensional, and the Hodge theorem gives the topological direct decomposition $H^{k-1}=\ker\Delta_{A_0}\oplus\Delta_{A_0}(H^{k+1})$. Thus its range is closed with codimension equal to the kernel dimension, so $\Delta_{A_0}$ is Fredholm of index zero. By [F6] and the compactness of $K_A$ from step 1.1, $\Delta_A=\Delta_{A_0}+K_A$ is Fredholm of index zero. [F3, F6, step 1.1]
  | 
  | 2.2 The smooth reference estimate [F4] and $\Delta_{A_0}=\Delta_A-K_A$ give $\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}\le C(\|\Delta_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}+\|K_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}+\|\xi\|_{L^2})$. The three terms in $K_A$ from step 1.1 satisfy $\|K_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}\le C_A\|\xi\|_{H^k}$. The Sobolev interpolation estimate $\|\xi\|_{H^k}\le\varepsilon\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}+C_\varepsilon\|\xi\|_{L^2}$ follows by finite-atlas localization and the estimate in [F5]. Choose $\varepsilon$ so that the resulting $H^{k+1}$ term is absorbed into the left side. This yields $\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}\le C_A'(\|\Delta_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}+\|\xi\|_{L^2})$. [F4, F5, F8, step 1.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 3.1 If $\Delta_A\xi=0$ for $\xi\in H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)$, the formal-adjoint identity, first for smooth approximants and then by Sobolev continuity, gives $0=\langle\Delta_A\xi,\xi\rangle_{L^2}=\|d_A\xi\|_{L^2}^2$. Hence $d_A\xi=0$. Parallel transport makes $\xi$ determined by $\xi(p_m)$, and that value commutes with holonomy. By [F2], $\exp(t\xi(p_m))$ lies in the finite group $Z_N$ for every real $t$, because $\xi(p_m)$ commutes with holonomy. The path is continuous and equals the identity at $t=0$, so it is constant; differentiating at zero gives $\xi(p_m)=0$. Parallel transport then gives $\xi=0$. [F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 4.1 Since $\Delta_A$ is Fredholm of index zero and its kernel is zero by step 3.1, its cokernel has dimension zero. Its range is closed by the Fredholm property, so $\Delta_A$ is onto and therefore bijective. [step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]
  | 
  | 4.2 If no constant bounded $\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}$ by $\|\Delta_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}$, choose $\|\xi_j\|_{H^{k+1}}=1$ with $\Delta_A\xi_j\to0$. By [F4], a subsequence converges in $L^2$; applying the estimate of step 2.2 to differences shows it is Cauchy in $H^{k+1}$. Its limit has $H^{k+1}$ norm one and lies in $\ker\Delta_A$, contradicting step 3.1. Thus $\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}\le C_A''\|\Delta_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}$. Together with bijectivity this proves the inverse $G_A$ is bounded. [F4, step 3.1, step 2.2]
  | 
  | 5.1 The operator-valued map $A\mapsto\Delta_A\in\mathcal B(H^{k+1},H^{k-1})$ is a continuous polynomial of degree at most two in the local coefficients of $A$, by step 1.1 and the product estimates [F8]. Near a fixed irreducible $A$, write $\Delta_{A+h}=\Delta_A+E(h)$; for small $h$, [F7] gives $(\Delta_{A+h})^{-1}=(I+G_AE(h))^{-1}G_A=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-G_AE(h))^nG_A$. On a neighborhood where $\|G_AE(h)\|\le\rho<1$, the Neumann series converges in operator norm. Its $m$-th derivative is bounded termwise by a constant times $n^m\rho^{n-m}$ for $n\ge m$, whose series converges; hence all derivatives converge uniformly on a smaller neighborhood. This proves that inversion and $A\mapsto G_A$ are smooth there. [F7, F8, step 1.1, step 4.2, algebra]
  | 
  | 6.1 Define $\Pi_A=d_AG_Ad_A^*$. The Sobolev orders give a bounded map $H^k(\Omega^1(\operatorname{ad}P))\to H^k(\Omega^1(\operatorname{ad}P))$. Since $\Delta_A G_A=I$ and $G_A\Delta_A=I$, one has $\Pi_A d_A\xi=d_A\xi$ and $d_A^*(I-\Pi_A)a=0$ for every $\xi\in H^{k+1}$ and $a\in H^k$. Thus $\Pi_A$ is a bounded projection with range $\operatorname{im}d_A$, while $I-\Pi_A$ has range $\ker d_A^*$. Their ranges intersect trivially: if $d_A\xi\in\ker d_A^*$, then $\Delta_A\xi=0$, so step 3.1 gives $\xi=0$. This proves the direct-sum decomposition and projection formula. Smooth dependence of $G_A$, $d_A$, and $d_A^*$ from step 5.1 makes $\Pi_A$ smooth in $A$. [F1, step 3.1, step 5.1, algebra] ∎
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | The three arrows below have the Sobolev domains and codomains written above them. Their composite is the bounded projection $\Pi_A=d_AG_Ad_A^*$ proved in step 6.1; the proof of its image and kernel is the operator argument in the proof, not the diagram.
  | 
  | ```tikzcd
  | H^k(\Omega^1(\operatorname{ad}P)) \arrow[r,"d_A^*"] & H^{k-1}(\operatorname{ad}P) \arrow[r,"G_A"] & H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P) \arrow[r,"d_A"] & H^k(\Omega^1(\operatorname{ad}P))
  | ```
REPAIR items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md: passes only after auto-repair — adopt the canonical form:
  | **Given:** Full AC; the compact Riemannian bundle data; an irreducible connection $A\in\mathcal A_k^*$; and $k>d/2+1$. For the $C^\infty$ clause, take the center $A$ in $\mathcal A_\infty^*$, as required to establish local triviality at every smooth point.
  | 
  | [A1] Full AC is the inherited axiom base for the Sobolev and Banach-manifold suppliers. [[def-axiom-of-choice]]
  | 
  | [F1] The Sobolev connection space is affine, the right gauge action is smooth, and its derivative at $(A,e)$ is $(a,\xi)\mapsto a+d_A\xi$. [[def-qftx-smooth-sobolev-gauge-configuration]], [[lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus]]
  | 
  | [F2] The reduced action is free and proper, and the irreducible locus is open. [[lem-qftx-gauge-stabilizer-and-properness]]
  | 
  | [F3] The Coulomb splitting is $H^k(T^*M\otimes\operatorname{ad}P)=\ker d_A^*\oplus\operatorname{im}d_A$, with bounded projection $d_AG_Ad_A^*$. [[lem-qftx-covariant-laplacian-sobolev-splitting]]
  | 
  | [F4] The Banach implicit-function theorem gives a local solution graph when the partial derivative in the solved variable is a bounded isomorphism; the Banach inverse-function theorem gives a local diffeomorphism when the full derivative is a bounded isomorphism. [[thm-implicit-function-theorem-for-banach-spaces]], [[thm-inverse-function-theorem-for-banach-spaces]]
  | 
  | [F5] A sufficiently small bounded perturbation of a bounded isomorphism remains invertible. [[lem-neumann-series-and-small-perturbations-of-bounded-inverses]]
  | 
  | [F6] For a smooth-coefficient elliptic operator on a compact manifold, distributional solutions gain two Sobolev derivatives when the right-hand side gains the corresponding Sobolev order, with the stated elliptic estimate. [[thm-elliptic-differential-operators-on-compact-vector-bundles-have-parametrices-and-regularity]]
  | 
  | [F7] For every integer $r>d/2$, multiplication is bounded on $H^r$, and the embeddings $H^s\hookrightarrow C^m$ hold when $s>d/2+m$; the finite-atlas estimates used in the smoothness bootstrap follow by localization and Hölder. [[cor-sobolev-algebra-above-the-critical-index]], [[thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding]]
  | 
  | 
  | 
  | **Given:** The hypotheses of the Statement.
  | 
  | 1.1 The finite center $Z_N$ acts freely on the Banach Lie group $\mathcal G_{k+1}$ by translations. Around any group element choose an open chart whose distinct $Z_N$-translates are disjoint; the quotient map restricts to a chart on each such neighborhood, with transition maps given by smooth translations. Multiplication and inversion descend smoothly, so $\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ is a Banach Lie group. Since a central gauge section acts trivially on connections, this is the structure group of the reduced action. [A1, F1, algebra]
  | 
  | 1.2 The tangent space of the Coulomb slice at $A$ is $\ker d_A^*$. By [F3], the bounded linear map $L_A:\ker d_A^*\oplus H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)\to H^k(T^*M\otimes\operatorname{ad}P)$, $L_A(a,\xi)=a+d_A\xi$, is an isomorphism with inverse $v\mapsto(v-d_AG_Ad_A^*v,G_Ad_A^*v)$. The derivative of $(a,\xi)\mapsto(A+a)^{\exp\xi}$ at $(0,0)$ is $L_A$ by [F1]. [F1, F3]
  | 
  | 1.3 Define $F(a,\xi):=d_A^*((A+a)^{\exp\xi}-A)$ for $a\in H^k(T^*M\otimes\operatorname{ad}P)$ near $0$ and $\xi\in H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)$ near $0$. This is a smooth map into $H^{k-1}(\operatorname{ad}P)$ by [F1], and $F(0,0)=0$. Its partial derivative in $\xi$ at $(0,0)$ is $d_A^*d_A=\Delta_A$, an isomorphism by [F3]. The implicit-function theorem [F4] therefore gives, for every connection sufficiently near $A$, a unique nearby gauge parameter moving it into the Coulomb condition $d_A^*(B^u-A)=0$. This supplies a local section of the orbit projection. [F1, F3, F4]
  | 
  | 2.1 Apply the inverse-function theorem [F4] to $\Phi_A(s,u):=s^u$ on the local Banach product $S_{A,\varepsilon}\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ near $(A,e)$; its derivative is $L_A$ from step 1.2. Shrink $\varepsilon$ so the slice remains in the open irreducible locus. At $(s,e)$, for $s=A+a$, the derivative is $L_s(b,\xi)=b+d_s\xi$ on $b\in\ker d_A^*$; this differs from $L_A$ only by $(b,\xi)\mapsto[a,\xi]$. Its operator norm is at most $C\|a\|_{H^k}$ by [F7]. Choose $\varepsilon$ so $C\varepsilon\|L_A^{-1}\|<1$; then [F5] makes $L_s$ an isomorphism for every $s$ in the slice. At an arbitrary $(s,u_0)$, write $u=u_0v$ near $u_0$ and use $s^{u_0v}=(s^{u_0})^v$. Applying the inverse action by $u_0$ to the target tangent and changing the gauge tangent by the invertible adjoint action identifies this derivative with $L_s$. Hence the derivative is an isomorphism at every point of the slice product, and the inverse-function theorem makes $\Phi_A$ a local diffeomorphism throughout. [F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, step 1.2]
  | 
  | 3.1 The map $\Phi_A$ is injective after possibly shrinking the slice further. Otherwise there would be sequences $s_j,t_j\in S_{A,1/j}$ and distinct pairs $(s_j,u_j)\ne(t_j,v_j)$ with $s_j^{u_j}=t_j^{v_j}$. Put $w_j=u_jv_j^{-1}$; then $s_j^{w_j}=t_j$ and $s_j,t_j\to A$. Properness [F2] gives a subsequence of $w_j$ converging to $w$ with $A^w=A$. Freeness [F2] gives $w=e$. Hence for all sufficiently large indices, both $(s_j,w_j)$ and $(t_j,e)$ lie in the local injectivity neighborhood from step 2.1. Their equal images force $s_j=t_j$ and $w_j=e$, and freeness then gives $u_j=v_j$, contradicting distinctness. [F2, step 2.1]
