[F3] A $C^1$ map between real Banach spaces whose derivative at a point is a bounded linear isomorphism is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism between suitable open neighborhoods. [[thm-inverse-function-theorem-for-banach-spaces]]

## Proof

1.1 On an overlap of two relatively compact bundle charts, a localized section changes coordinates by multiplication by a smooth transition matrix and by a smooth coordinate change. Every derivative of order at most $k$ of the transformed coefficient is a finite sum of bounded derivatives of these transition maps times derivatives of the original coefficient of order at most $k$. The Jacobians and inverse Jacobians are bounded on the compact supports. The product and chain rules therefore bound each localized $H^k$ norm by a constant times the finite-atlas norm in the other chart. Summing over the finite cover gives both norm inequalities. Thus the norms are equivalent and define the same complete Sobolev topology. [given, A1, F1, algebra]

1.2 Localize by the fixed finite partition and extend each compactly supported coefficient by zero into a bounded Euclidean ball. For $p=2$, $2k>d$; [F2] makes each localized $H^k$ coefficient a Banach algebra and gives its $C^1$ representative when $k>d/2+1$. The finite sum of these estimates proves the same algebra and embedding bounds for sections, matrix-valued functions, and forms in the chosen bundle atlas. [F1, F2, step 1.1]

2.1 The finite-dimensional matrix exponential is smooth, has derivative the identity at zero on $\mathfrak{su}(N)$, and maps $\mathfrak{su}(N)$ into $SU(N)$. In the Banach algebra from step 1.2 its power series and every termwise derivative converge in $H^{k+1}$ on a sufficiently small ball, since the algebra norm is submultiplicative after an equivalent rescaling. The matrix logarithm series converges on a neighborhood of the identity; for a unitary matrix sufficiently close to identity its logarithm is anti-Hermitian, and its trace is zero because the determinant is one and the sum of the small eigenvalue angles lies in $2\pi\mathbb Z$. Hence pointwise $\exp$ and $\log$ give inverse smooth maps between neighborhoods of zero in $H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)$ and the identity in $\mathcal G_{k+1}$. The inverse-function theorem [F3] also supplies these local Banach charts. Their translated charts cover the group; changes of chart are smooth because the transition functions of $\operatorname{Ad}P$ act by smooth conjugation and the multiplication is a bounded bilinear Sobolev map. The pointwise product and inverse therefore close on $\mathcal G_{k+1}$ and are smooth, giving the asserted Hilbert Lie group. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.2]

3.1 In a local trivialization the action is $A^u=u^{-1}Au+u^{-1}du$. Step 1.2 bounds multiplication $H^{k+1}\times H^k\to H^k$ because $H^{k+1}\hookrightarrow H^k$ continuously and $H^k$ is an algebra. Thus the first term is smooth in $(A,u)$. In the charts of step 2.1, inversion is smooth and differentiation $H^{k+1}\to H^k$ is bounded. The derivative of the second term $f(u)=u^{-1}du$ in a tangent direction $\eta$ is $Df(u)[\eta]=-u^{-1}\eta u^{-1}du+u^{-1}d\eta$. Every further derivative is a finite sum of products of $u^{-1}$, tangent factors $\eta_i$, and at most one differentiated tangent factor $d\eta_j$; each product is bounded in $H^k$ by step 1.2. Finite-dimensional Taylor remainders for matrix inversion, multiplied using the same algebra estimate, tend to zero in the required Sobolev norm at every order. Therefore $f$ and the full gauge action are smooth. [F1, step 1.2, step 2.1, algebra]

4.1 For $t\in\mathbb R$, expand in the finite-dimensional matrix algebra: $e^{-t\xi}(A+a)e^{t\xi}=A+a+t[A,\xi]+o(t)$ in $H^k$, while $e^{-t\xi}d(e^{t\xi})=t\,d\xi+o(t)$ in $H^k$ by the bounded derivative and product estimates of step 1.2. Adding the two terms gives derivative $a+d\xi+[A,\xi]=a+d_A\xi$, which is the displayed formula. [F1, step 1.2, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
