94:4.1 For each $1\le r<N$, represent an $r$-plane in $\mathbb C^N$ by its Hermitian orthogonal projection $Q$, so the Grassmannian is $\{Q:Q^*=Q,\ Q^2=Q,\ \operatorname{tr}Q=r\}$ in the finite-dimensional matrix space. Such projections have norm one. Regard any sequence of them as constant curves on $[0,1]$; [F3] gives a uniformly convergent subsequence, and the three defining equations pass to its limit. Thus each Grassmannian is compact. Suppose $A_j\to A$ and each $A_j$ is reducible. A subsequence has a fixed invariant dimension $r$, and by compactness another subsequence of invariant projections converges to $Q$. For every fixed based loop $\gamma$, [F2] and [F1] give $\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A_j,\gamma)\to\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A,\gamma)$. Since the holonomy matrices are unitary and preserve $\operatorname{ran}Q_j$, they commute with $Q_j$; passing to the limit shows that $\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A,\gamma)$ commutes with $Q$ for every loop. Therefore $\operatorname{ran}Q$ is a nonzero proper invariant subspace and $A$ is reducible. The reducible locus is closed, so $\mathcal A_k^*$ is open. [F1, F2, F3, step 3.1, algebra]
96:5.1 The action map is continuous by the preceding Sobolev-calculus lemma. For a compact subset of $\mathcal A_k^*\times\mathcal A_k^*$, any sequence in its inverse image has a subsequence whose connection and transformed-connection coordinates converge in that compact set. Steps 1.2–3.2 then give a further subsequence of gauge classes converging in $\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$. The inverse image is closed because the action map is continuous, so sequential compactness in these metrizable spaces is compactness. This proves properness. By step 3.1 the reduced action is free. [F1, step 3.1, step 1.2, step 2.2] ∎

