items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:110:4.1 Let $U_A:=\Phi_A(S_{A,\varepsilon}\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1})$. It is open because $\Phi_A$ is a local diffeomorphism at every point of its domain, and it is exactly the saturation of the slice. By steps 3.1 and 2.1, $\Phi_A$ is a bijective local diffeomorphism onto $U_A$, hence a diffeomorphism. If $V_A:=\pi_k(S_{A,\varepsilon})$, then $\pi_k^{-1}(V_A)=U_A$. For every open subset $O$ of the slice, $O\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ is open and its image under $\Phi_A$ is open; thus $\pi_k(O)$ is open in $V_A$ by the quotient topology. Therefore the slice-to-quotient map is a smooth chart and $\Phi_A$ is an equivariant local principal trivialization. [step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-stabilizer-and-properness.md:92:3.1 On the standard $\mathbb C^N$, the holonomy matrices are unitary. If the representation is irreducible, [F5] makes every matrix commuting with the representation a scalar $\lambda I_N$. Membership in $SU(N)$ gives $|\lambda|=1$ and $\lambda^N=1$, hence its centralizer is $Z_N$. The stabilizer result of steps 1.1–1.2 therefore gives full stabilizer $Z_N$ and trivial reduced stabilizer. [F5, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
items/ex-qftx-coulomb-chart-linearization.md:80:2.1 Set $Q_A=d_AG_Ad_A^*$, the bounded projection onto $\operatorname{im}d_A$ from [F2]. For any $v\in H^k$, $d_A^*(v-Q_Av)=d_A^*v-d_A^*d_AG_Ad_A^*v=0$ and $L_A(v-Q_Av,G_Ad_A^*v)=v$. Conversely, for $a\in\ker d_A^*$ and $\xi\in H^{k+1}$, applying the proposed inverse to $a+d_A\xi$ gives $G_Ad_A^*(a+d_A\xi)=G_A(d_A^*a+d_A^*d_A\xi)=\xi$ and first component $a$. Both composites are therefore identities with the Sobolev orders supplied by [F2], proving the stated bounded inverse. The chart interpretation is the one established in [F3]. [F2, F3, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:106:2.1 Apply the inverse-function theorem [F4] to $\Phi_A(s,u):=s^u$ on the local Banach product $S_{A,\varepsilon}\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ near $(A,e)$; its derivative is $L_A$ from step 1.2. Shrink $\varepsilon$ so the slice remains in the open irreducible locus. At $(s,e)$, for $s=A+a$, the derivative is $L_s(b,\xi)=b+d_s\xi$ on $b\in\ker d_A^*$; this differs from $L_A$ only by $(b,\xi)\mapsto[a,\xi]$. Its operator norm is at most $C\|a\|_{H^k}$ by [F7]. Choose $\varepsilon$ so $C\varepsilon\|L_A^{-1}\|<1$; then [F5] makes $L_s$ an isomorphism for every $s$ in the slice. At an arbitrary $(s,u_0)$, write $u=u_0v$ near $u_0$ and use $s^{u_0v}=(s^{u_0})^v$. Applying the inverse action by $u_0$ to the target tangent and changing the gauge tangent by the invertible adjoint action identifies this derivative with $L_s$. Hence the derivative is an isomorphism at every point of the slice product, and the inverse-function theorem makes $\Phi_A$ a local diffeomorphism throughout. [F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, step 1.2]
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:108:3.1 The map $\Phi_A$ is injective after possibly shrinking the slice further. Otherwise there would be sequences $s_j,t_j\in S_{A,1/j}$ and distinct pairs $(s_j,u_j)\ne(t_j,v_j)$ with $s_j^{u_j}=t_j^{v_j}$. Put $w_j=u_jv_j^{-1}$; then $s_j^{w_j}=t_j$ and $s_j,t_j\to A$. Properness [F2] gives a subsequence of $w_j$ converging to $w$ with $A^w=A$. Freeness [F2] gives $w=e$. Hence for all sufficiently large indices, both $(s_j,w_j)$ and $(t_j,e)$ lie in the local injectivity neighborhood from step 2.1. Their equal images force $s_j=t_j$ and $w_j=e$, and freeness then gives $u_j=v_j$, contradicting distinctness. [F2, step 2.1]
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:110:4.1 Let $U_A:=\Phi_A(S_{A,\varepsilon}\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1})$. It is open because $\Phi_A$ is a local diffeomorphism at every point of its domain, and it is exactly the saturation of the slice. By steps 3.1 and 2.1, $\Phi_A$ is a bijective local diffeomorphism onto $U_A$, hence a diffeomorphism. If $V_A:=\pi_k(S_{A,\varepsilon})$, then $\pi_k^{-1}(V_A)=U_A$. For every open subset $O$ of the slice, $O\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ is open and its image under $\Phi_A$ is open; thus $\pi_k(O)$ is open in $V_A$ by the quotient topology. Therefore the slice-to-quotient map is a smooth chart and $\Phi_A$ is an equivariant local principal trivialization. [step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:112:5.1 The orbit relation on $\mathcal A_k^*$ is closed: if $x_j^{u_j}=y_j$ and $(x_j,y_j)\to(x,y)$, properness [F2] gives a convergent subsequence $u_{j_\ell}\to u$, and continuity of the action gives $x^u=y$. For distinct orbits, the complement of this closed relation contains their pair, so there are product neighborhoods $O_x\times O_y$ disjoint from the relation. Their saturations are open and disjoint: if the saturations intersected, some translate of a point in $O_x$ would equal a translate of a point in $O_y$, making that pair related. Their quotient images therefore separate the two orbits. This proves the quotient is Hausdorff. [F1, F2, step 4.1]
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:114:5.2 For the smooth-space clause, fix now a smooth irreducible center $A\in\mathcal A_\infty^*$ and choose its Banach slice from steps 2–3. Put $U_A=\Phi_A(S_{A,\varepsilon}\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1})$ and $V_A=\pi_k(S_{A,\varepsilon})$. Since $U_A$ is open in $\mathcal A_k^*$, $U_A\cap\mathcal A_\infty^*$ is open in the stated $C^\infty$ topology; set $S_A^\infty=S_{A,\varepsilon}\cap\mathcal A_\infty^*$ and $V_A^\infty=\pi_\infty(S_A^\infty)$. For a smooth $B\in U_A$, its unique reduced gauge class from step 4.1 has a representative $u\in\mathcal G_{k+1}$. It satisfies $d_A^*(B^{u^{-1}}-A)=0$. Locally in a bundle frame, write $C_j=uB_j u^{-1}-(\partial_j u)u^{-1}-A_j$. Substitution in $d_A^*C=0$, use of $\partial_i(u^{-1})=-u^{-1}(\partial_i u)u^{-1}$, and multiplication on the right by $u$ isolate the equation $g^{ij}\partial_i\partial_j u=R(A,B,g;u,u^{-1},du)$ on each chart. Every term in $R$ is a finite product of smooth coefficients (including first derivatives of $A,B$) and factors among $u,u^{-1},du$; hence the principal operator is the scalar smooth Laplacian on each matrix entry. If $u\in H^{r+1}$ for $r\ge k$, then this right-hand side lies in $H^r$ by [F7] and the smooth coefficient bounds. Elliptic regularity [F6] gives $u\in H^{r+2}$. Iteration proves $u$ smooth; changing its representative by an element of the finite center preserves smoothness. Thus every smooth point of the Banach saturation has a smooth trivialization representative, and $U_A\cap\mathcal A_\infty^*=\pi_\infty^{-1}(V_A^\infty)$. [F1, F2, F6, F7, step 4.1]
items/thm-qftx-sobolev-smooth-irreducible-gauge-bundle.md:116:6.1 Suppose smooth $B_j\to B$ in $C^\infty$ within $U_A\cap\mathcal A_\infty^*$. The Banach trivialization from step 4.1 gives convergence of their slice representatives and reduced gauge classes in $H^{k+1}$. In a quotient chart around the limiting class from step 1.1, choose lifts $u_j,u$ that converge in $H^{k+1}$; this is possible because the finite center acts by disjoint translates. The equation in step 5.2 and elliptic estimates [F6] bootstrap convergence: if $u_j\to u$ in $H^{r+1}$, then the assumed $C^\infty$ convergence gives $B_j\to B$ in $H^{r+1}$, and [F7] makes the right-hand sides converge in $H^r$. Apply the elliptic estimate to $u_j-u$, using also their already known $H^{k+1}$ convergence for the $L^2$ term, to get $u_j\to u$ in $H^{r+2}$. Induction gives convergence in every Sobolev order, which on compact $M$ is the $C^\infty$ section topology. The slice representatives converge in $C^\infty$ by their action formula. Conversely the forward action is continuous in the $C^\infty$ topology by multiplication and differentiation of smooth sections. The saturation $U_A\cap\mathcal A_\infty^*$ is open, and $V_A^\infty$ is open in the quotient because the quotient map is open: the saturation of any open set is a union of its open gauge translates. The restricted map $S_A^\infty\times\bar{\mathcal G}_\infty\to\pi_\infty^{-1}(V_A^\infty)$ is therefore a homeomorphism for the actual quotient topology, and it is equivariant. This proves the smooth-space topological principal bundle claim. [F1, F6, F7, step 1.1, step 4.1, step 5.2] ∎
items/lem-qftx-covariant-laplacian-sobolev-splitting.md:98:2.1 Apply [F3] with $r=k-1\ge0$ to $\Delta_{A_0}:H^{k+1}\to H^{k-1}$. Its kernel is finite dimensional, and the Hodge theorem gives the topological direct decomposition $H^{k-1}=\ker\Delta_{A_0}\oplus\Delta_{A_0}(H^{k+1})$. Thus its range is closed with codimension equal to the kernel dimension, so $\Delta_{A_0}$ is Fredholm of index zero. By [F6] and the compactness of $K_A$ from step 1.1, $\Delta_A=\Delta_{A_0}+K_A$ is Fredholm of index zero. [F3, F6, step 1.1]
items/lem-qftx-covariant-laplacian-sobolev-splitting.md:100:2.2 The smooth reference estimate [F4] and $\Delta_{A_0}=\Delta_A-K_A$ give $\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}\le C(\|\Delta_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}+\|K_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}+\|\xi\|_{L^2})$. The three terms in $K_A$ from step 1.1 satisfy $\|K_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}\le C_A\|\xi\|_{H^k}$. The Sobolev interpolation estimate $\|\xi\|_{H^k}\le\varepsilon\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}+C_\varepsilon\|\xi\|_{L^2}$ follows by finite-atlas localization and the estimate in [F5]. Choose $\varepsilon$ so that the resulting $H^{k+1}$ term is absorbed into the left side. This yields $\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}\le C_A'(\|\Delta_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}+\|\xi\|_{L^2})$. [F4, F5, F8, step 1.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-covariant-laplacian-sobolev-splitting.md:102:3.1 If $\Delta_A\xi=0$ for $\xi\in H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)$, the formal-adjoint identity, first for smooth approximants and then by Sobolev continuity, gives $0=\langle\Delta_A\xi,\xi\rangle_{L^2}=\|d_A\xi\|_{L^2}^2$. Hence $d_A\xi=0$. Parallel transport makes $\xi$ determined by $\xi(p_m)$, and that value commutes with holonomy. By [F2], $\exp(t\xi(p_m))$ lies in the finite group $Z_N$ for every real $t$, because $\xi(p_m)$ commutes with holonomy. The path is continuous and equals the identity at $t=0$, so it is constant; differentiating at zero gives $\xi(p_m)=0$. Parallel transport then gives $\xi=0$. [F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-covariant-laplacian-sobolev-splitting.md:104:4.1 Since $\Delta_A$ is Fredholm of index zero and its kernel is zero by step 3.1, its cokernel has dimension zero. Its range is closed by the Fredholm property, so $\Delta_A$ is onto and therefore bijective. [step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-covariant-laplacian-sobolev-splitting.md:106:4.2 If no constant bounded $\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}$ by $\|\Delta_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}$, choose $\|\xi_j\|_{H^{k+1}}=1$ with $\Delta_A\xi_j\to0$. By [F4], a subsequence converges in $L^2$; applying the estimate of step 2.2 to differences shows it is Cauchy in $H^{k+1}$. Its limit has $H^{k+1}$ norm one and lies in $\ker\Delta_A$, contradicting step 3.1. Thus $\|\xi\|_{H^{k+1}}\le C_A''\|\Delta_A\xi\|_{H^{k-1}}$. Together with bijectivity this proves the inverse $G_A$ is bounded. [F4, step 3.1, step 2.2]
items/lem-qftx-covariant-laplacian-sobolev-splitting.md:108:5.1 The operator-valued map $A\mapsto\Delta_A\in\mathcal B(H^{k+1},H^{k-1})$ is a continuous polynomial of degree at most two in the local coefficients of $A$, by step 1.1 and the product estimates [F8]. Near a fixed irreducible $A$, write $\Delta_{A+h}=\Delta_A+E(h)$; for small $h$, [F7] gives $(\Delta_{A+h})^{-1}=(I+G_AE(h))^{-1}G_A=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(-G_AE(h))^nG_A$. On a neighborhood where $\|G_AE(h)\|\le\rho<1$, the Neumann series converges in operator norm. Its $m$-th derivative is bounded termwise by a constant times $n^m\rho^{n-m}$ for $n\ge m$, whose series converges; hence all derivatives converge uniformly on a smaller neighborhood. This proves that inversion and $A\mapsto G_A$ are smooth there. [F7, F8, step 1.1, step 4.2, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-covariant-laplacian-sobolev-splitting.md:110:6.1 Define $\Pi_A=d_AG_Ad_A^*$. The Sobolev orders give a bounded map $H^k(\Omega^1(\operatorname{ad}P))\to H^k(\Omega^1(\operatorname{ad}P))$. Since $\Delta_A G_A=I$ and $G_A\Delta_A=I$, one has $\Pi_A d_A\xi=d_A\xi$ and $d_A^*(I-\Pi_A)a=0$ for every $\xi\in H^{k+1}$ and $a\in H^k$. Thus $\Pi_A$ is a bounded projection with range $\operatorname{im}d_A$, while $I-\Pi_A$ has range $\ker d_A^*$. Their ranges intersect trivially: if $d_A\xi\in\ker d_A^*$, then $\Delta_A\xi=0$, so step 3.1 gives $\xi=0$. This proves the direct-sum decomposition and projection formula. Smooth dependence of $G_A$, $d_A$, and $d_A^*$ from step 5.1 makes $\Pi_A$ smooth in $A$. [F1, step 3.1, step 5.1, algebra] ∎
items/lem-qftx-covariant-laplacian-sobolev-splitting.md:114:The three arrows below have the Sobolev domains and codomains written above them. Their composite is the bounded projection $\Pi_A=d_AG_Ad_A^*$ proved in step 5.1; the proof of its image and kernel is the operator argument in the proof, not the diagram.
items/ex-qftx-covariantly-constant-stabilizer.md:62:2.1 If $A$ is irreducible, [F2] identifies the full stabilizer with $Z_N$. Every element of this center is divided out in $\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$, so the reduced stabilizer consists only of the identity coset. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
items/lem-qftx-gauge-stabilizer-and-properness.md:88:2.1 Conversely, let $g\in SU(N)$ commute with every element of $\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A)$. Transport $g$ from $p_m$ along any path to a point $x$ and conjugate it by the path's parallel-transport matrix. Two paths give the same value because their concatenation has holonomy commuting with $g$. In local coordinates this gives a parallel section $u$ with $u(p_m)=g$. It is $C^1$ since $A\in H^k\hookrightarrow C^1$ and the parameter-dependent parallel-transport ODE is $C^1$ in the endpoint. The equation $du=uA-Au$, the bounds [F6], and induction on $r=0,1,\ldots,k$ show $u\in H^{k+1}$: starting with its $C^1$ local coefficients, if $u\in H^r$ then the right side is in $H^r$, so all first derivatives are in $H^r$ and $u\in H^{r+1}$. Thus $u\in\mathcal G_{k+1}$ and stabilizes $A$, proving that evaluation has exactly the stated image. [given, F1, F2, F6, step 1.1]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-stabilizer-and-properness.md:90:2.2 By [F4], a subsequence converges strongly in $H^k$ to a section $u$. The $H^k\hookrightarrow C^0$ embedding preserves the pointwise $SU(N)$ equations and the bundle transition law, so $u$ is a group-bundle section. In the gauge equation, $A_j\to A$, $B_j\to B$, and $u_j\to u$ in $H^k$; because $H^k$ is an algebra, $u_jB_j-A_ju_j\to uB-Au$ in $H^k$. Thus $du_j$ converges in $H^k$ and the local coefficients converge in $H^{k+1}$. The limit satisfies $du=uB-Au$, equivalently $B=A^u$, and $u\in\mathcal G_{k+1}$. [F1, F2, F4, step 1.2]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-stabilizer-and-properness.md:92:3.1 On the standard $\mathbb C^N$, the holonomy matrices are unitary. If the representation is irreducible, [F5] makes every matrix commuting with the representation a scalar $\lambda I_N$. Membership in $SU(N)$ gives $|\lambda|=1$ and $\lambda^N=1$, hence its centralizer is $Z_N$. The stabilizer result of steps 1.1–1.2 therefore gives full stabilizer $Z_N$ and trivial reduced stabilizer. [F5, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-stabilizer-and-properness.md:94:4.1 For each $1\le r<N$, represent an $r$-plane in $\mathbb C^N$ by its Hermitian orthogonal projection $Q$, so the Grassmannian is $\{Q:Q^*=Q,\ Q^2=Q,\ \operatorname{tr}Q=r\}$ in the finite-dimensional matrix space. Such projections have norm one. Regard any sequence of them as constant curves on $[0,1]$; [F3] gives a uniformly convergent subsequence, and the three defining equations pass to its limit. Thus each Grassmannian is compact. Suppose $A_j\to A$ and each $A_j$ is reducible. A subsequence has a fixed invariant dimension $r$, and by compactness another subsequence of invariant projections converges to $Q$. For every fixed based loop $\gamma$, [F2] and [F1] give $\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A_j,\gamma)\to\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A,\gamma)$. Since the holonomy matrices are unitary and preserve $\operatorname{ran}Q_j$, they commute with $Q_j$; passing to the limit shows that $\operatorname{Hol}_{p_m}(A,\gamma)$ commutes with $Q$ for every loop. Therefore $\operatorname{ran}Q$ is a nonzero proper invariant subspace and $A$ is reducible. The reducible locus is closed, so $\mathcal A_k^*$ is open. [F1, F2, F3, step 3.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-stabilizer-and-properness.md:96:5.1 The action map is continuous by the preceding Sobolev-calculus lemma. For a compact subset of $\mathcal A_k^*\times\mathcal A_k^*$, any sequence in its inverse image has a subsequence whose connection and transformed-connection coordinates converge in that compact set. Steps 1.2–3.2 then give a further subsequence of gauge classes converging in $\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$. The inverse image is closed because the action map is continuous, so sequential compactness in these metrizable spaces is compactness. This proves properness. By step 3.1 the reduced action is free. [F1, step 3.1, step 1.2, step 2.2] ∎
items/lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus.md:72:2.1 Localize by the fixed finite partition and extend each compactly supported coefficient by zero into a bounded Euclidean ball. For $p=2$, $2k>d$; [F2] makes each localized $H^k$ coefficient a Banach algebra and gives its $C^1$ representative when $k>d/2+1$. The finite sum of these estimates proves the same algebra and embedding bounds for sections, matrix-valued functions, and forms in the chosen bundle atlas. [F1, F2, step 1.1]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus.md:74:3.1 The finite-dimensional matrix exponential is smooth, has derivative the identity at zero on $\mathfrak{su}(N)$, and maps $\mathfrak{su}(N)$ into $SU(N)$. In the Banach algebra from step 2.1 its power series and every termwise derivative converge in $H^{k+1}$ on a sufficiently small ball, since the algebra norm is submultiplicative after an equivalent rescaling. The matrix logarithm series converges on a neighborhood of the identity; for a unitary matrix sufficiently close to identity its logarithm is anti-Hermitian, and its trace is zero because the determinant is one and the sum of the small eigenvalue angles lies in $2\pi\mathbb Z$. Hence pointwise $\exp$ and $\log$ give inverse smooth maps between neighborhoods of zero in $H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)$ and the identity in $\mathcal G_{k+1}$. The inverse-function theorem [F3] also supplies these local Banach charts. Their translated charts cover the group; changes of chart are smooth because the transition functions of $\operatorname{Ad}P$ act by smooth conjugation and the multiplication is a bounded bilinear Sobolev map. The pointwise product and inverse therefore close on $\mathcal G_{k+1}$ and are smooth, giving the asserted Hilbert Lie group. [F1, F2, F3, step 2.1]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus.md:76:4.1 In a local trivialization the action is $A^u=u^{-1}Au+u^{-1}du$. Step 2.1 bounds multiplication $H^{k+1}\times H^k\to H^k$ because $H^{k+1}\hookrightarrow H^k$ continuously and $H^k$ is an algebra. Thus the first term is smooth in $(A,u)$. In the charts of step 3.1, inversion is smooth and differentiation $H^{k+1}\to H^k$ is bounded. The derivative of the second term $f(u)=u^{-1}du$ in a tangent direction $\eta$ is $Df(u)[\eta]=-u^{-1}\eta u^{-1}du+u^{-1}d\eta$. Every further derivative is a finite sum of products of $u^{-1}$, tangent factors $\eta_i$, and at most one differentiated tangent factor $d\eta_j$; each product is bounded in $H^k$ by step 2.1. Finite-dimensional Taylor remainders for matrix inversion, multiplied using the same algebra estimate, tend to zero in the required Sobolev norm at every order. Therefore $f$ and the full gauge action are smooth. [F1, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]
items/lem-qftx-gauge-sobolev-calculus.md:78:5.1 For $t\in\mathbb R$, expand in the finite-dimensional matrix algebra: $e^{-t\xi}(A+a)e^{t\xi}=A+a+t[A,\xi]+o(t)$ in $H^k$, while $e^{-t\xi}d(e^{t\xi})=t\,d\xi+o(t)$ in $H^k$ by the bounded derivative and product estimates of step 2.1. Adding the two terms gives derivative $a+d\xi+[A,\xi]=a+d_A\xi$, which is the displayed formula. [F1, step 2.1, step 4.1, algebra] ∎

