research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/gauge-slice-source-repair/preimages/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-3.notes.md:27:2. P. K. Mitter and C. M. Viallet, “On the Bundle of Connections and the Gauge Orbit Manifold in Yang–Mills Theory,” complete 16-page paper read at https://scispace.com/pdf/on-the-bundle-of-connections-and-the-gauge-orbit-manifold-in-1nks9qpdkj.pdf. Read §§I–V, printed pp. 458–472, especially §§IV.B–C, pp. 466–468, and §V, pp. 468–471. Its local slice theorem uses a Sobolev completion, compact connected semisimple matrix group, compact closed oriented Riemannian base, order above dimension/2+1, and free-action/irreducibility conditions. It is not a continuum measure proof and is not imported as the finite theorem's proof. Direct attempt to retrieve the earlier lpthe URL returned 404; the alternate full text was successfully inspected via the web PDF view. The required source-fetch-check later returned zero bytes on all five permitted attempts to the cited mirror (after the earlier 404), so it has no fetch_verified stamp. This source remains contextual rather than a proof supplier; the complete finite-dimensional proofs are local. No further fetch attempt will be made.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/gauge-slice-source-repair/preimages/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-3.notes.md:28:3. I. M. Singer, “Some Remarks on the Gribov Ambiguity,” Communications in Mathematical Physics 60 (1978), 7–12, DOI https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01609471. The historical note reports abstract/catalogue and introductory lines only; the full proof and exact hypotheses were not read. Five failures are preserved from the initial source-resolution pass: Project Euclid interstitials and two Turin PDF failures. During an unrelated alternative-source search for Mitter–Viallet, a search result surfaced the exact Project Euclid PDF URL https://projecteuclid.org/journals/communications-in-mathematical-physics/volume-60/issue-1/Some-remarks-on-the-Gribov-ambiguity/cmp/1103904019.pdf. Opening it returned a one-line HTML iframe wrapper; a batched screenshot request for its six pages failed because the tool did not resolve an application/pdf document. Those two later failed access routes are also recorded in batch-3.coverage.json. The exact earlier Turin URL and attempt times were not retained in the historical note; the coverage record preserves that uncertainty. The abstract's broad claim is insufficient to state or prove the assigned theorem. This remains an owner escalation; no Singer claim is treated as a proved supplier or as an open problem. No further Singer retrieval attempts will be made.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/gauge-slice-source-repair/preimages/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-3.notes.md:35:- The required source-fetch-check --stamp returned exit 1: Skinner passed with 4,537,043 bytes, 31 PDF pages, SHA-256 prefix 1d9bd7175a6577ef; the Mitter mirror returned zero bytes on five fetch attempts after the earlier 404; Singer was skipped by source-resolution escalation. Overall result: 1/3 sources stamped. No further attempts will be made for either unresolved source. Full Mitter text was read via the web PDF view, but its fetch stamp remains missing.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/gauge-slice-source-repair/preimages/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-3.notes.md:40:The finite-dimensional definition, local theorem, two examples, both frontmatter-only pages, batch manifest, proof contracts, source coverage, cross-batch dependency array, and authoring notes are complete for the available carriers. The planned Singer theorem remains unmaterialized because its exact hypotheses and complete proof were not recoverable; the source-resolution record documents the earlier five failures and two later unsuccessful access routes. The Mitter fetch stamp is also unresolved despite complete web-view reading. Both require owner disposition before treating the full A-page plan as complete.
