## Exact final target and smallest supplier interfaces

For each N ≥ 2 and every fixed smooth principal SU(N) bundle P over S4, also S3, give the affine space A of smooth connections and its gauge group G their C-infinity section topologies and A/G the quotient topology. There is no continuous right inverse of A → A/G. In fact the projection from the irreducible locus A* to A*/(G/Z_N) has no section. Irreducibility here means irreducibility of the holonomy representation on C^N; it must not be silently replaced by full SU(N) holonomy for N > 2. Singer announces a compact connected semisimple generalization, but writes the proof for SU(N). If a broader group class was approved independently, its central-cover/simple-factor reduction is an additional explicit proof obligation; it is not discharged by the abstract.

The complete concluding deduction is short once the missing suppliers exist: the irreducible projection is a principal G/Z_N bundle; a section trivializes it and retracts its structure group from A*; weak contractibility of A* would force that group to have trivial homotopy, contradicting the sphere gauge-group calculation. A putative section over all A/G restricts to the saturated irreducible subset. The proposal retains exactly these obligations:

- `qftx-sobolev-gauge-orbit-geometry`: explicit Sobolev/smooth configuration spaces; finite-atlas product and composition calculus; stabilizer centralizers and properness; rough-coefficient covariant Green inverse and Coulomb splitting; small globally effective slices; the C-infinity locally trivial principal bundle. Use integer k > dim(M)/2 + 1 and G of order k+1 acting on connections of order k. Existing smooth-coefficient elliptic estimates need a real rough-coefficient perturbation/bootstrap argument. Smooth inverse-limit compatibility and continuity in every seminorm also require proof.
- `qftx-irreducible-connection-topology`: finite holonomy witnesses and their openness; relative finite-parameter avoidance of reducibles; weak contractibility by affine disk extension and relative repair. The constructive holonomy-incidence argument below avoids assuming an unproved infinite-dimensional stratification theorem.
- `qftx-gauge-group-sphere-homotopy`: relative parametric smoothing of gauge sections; actual evaluation fibrations and finite-center coverings; hemisphere-clutching computation of based gauge homotopy; the smallest needed low SU(N) groups; nontrivial reduced-group homotopy; the general section/retract obstruction. The selected smaller route uses pi5 SU(3) nonzero from SU(2) → SU(3) → S5 and finiteness of pi4 SU(2), then sphere-quotient stabilization for higher N. Evaluation and finite-center coverings inject the resulting pi1 of the based gauge group into that of the reduced gauge group. For SU(2) it needs only pi5(S3) nonzero, instead of a full pi6(S3) = Z12 calculation. These unstable local inputs remain authoring obligations; neither pi4 SU(N) vanishing nor the boundary map onto pi4 SU(2) is needed.

For the last branch, evaluation yields pi1 G_m ≅ pi1 G because pi1 SU(2) = pi2 SU(2) = 0; hemisphere clutching gives pi1 G_m ≅ pi5(S3) = Z2; the finite covering G → G/Z2 injects pi1. This works for every bundle sector. The suggested pi0 shortcut is invalid: quotienting by the center need not preserve components. Singer's Theorem 6 explicitly distinguishes odd index, where the reduced group is connected, from even index. Theorem 7's proof on p. 11 explicitly supplies the pi5(S3) value used by the smaller route. No full unstable-group proof is supplied by that line.

## Constructive avoidance route assessed

For each k-plane in C^N, the stabilizer H_k = S(U(k)×U(N−k)) has codimension 2k(N−k) in SU(N). For r based loops the incidence manifold of tuples preserving such a plane is a bundle over Gr_C(k,N) with fibre H_k^r. Its dimension deficit in SU(N)^r is 2(r−1)k(N−k), growing arbitrarily with r.

A finite collection of holonomies witnesses every connection in the compact irreducible boundary family uniformly, using Grassmannian compactness. Give those loops small disjoint control arcs, unused by the other loops, by sufficiently small detours preserving the uniform irreducibility margin; add extra loops as needed. Bump one-forms along the arcs, with Lie-algebra coefficients transported by the original connection, make the derivative of the r-holonomy map onto SU(N)^r surjective in finite perturbation parameters. This is an explicit ODE variation calculation. The forms may depend smoothly on interior disk parameters; local normal extensions supply genuine global adjoint-valued forms.

Smooth the affine disk family's finite-dimensional parameter dependence in the interior while retaining an irreducible collar and the original continuous boundary exactly. Pulling back each incidence manifold along the submersive holonomy map gives dimension `disk_dimension + perturbation_dimension − 2(r−1)k(N−k)`. Choose r so that this is smaller than perturbation dimension for every k. Sard then makes the union of projected bad parameters null, so arbitrarily small good parameters exist. A cutoff fixes the boundary collar. Where the cutoff is positive the normalized holonomy derivative must remain invertible uniformly; where it vanishes the original family was already irreducible. Relative simplicial induction handles general finite polyhedra.

The dimension count and the logical implication are checked. Authors still owe the actual loop-detour construction, the ODE variation/submersion estimates uniformly over the compact family, parameter smoothing in the smooth-section topology, and the relative cutoff estimates. The JSON gives these as explicit proof steps. It does not pass off an unproved stratified avoidance slogan as a local proof.

## Reused library and consumers

The actual pinned gauge-group definition explicitly assigns **no topology**. Gauge action/connection suppliers supply finite-dimensional smooth geometry; they do not construct the infinite-dimensional gauge-orbit principal bundle. The imported compact Laplace-type Green theorem explicitly assumes smooth coefficients. The actual Banach-valued inverse/implicit function theorems are suitable; the split-surjective theorem with finite-dimensional target alone is insufficient. The ordinary smooth/continuous homotopy-category comparison does not by itself prove a parametric weak equivalence of section spaces. The generic fibration LES and numerable-bundle theorem require the actual bundle/fibration hypotheses before use. The proposal pins these exact candidates and does not edit their imported bytes. The inspected Schur theorem for complex unitary representations supplies scalar centralizers; the general division-ring Schur corollary alone does not supply that conclusion. Exact continuous/smooth parameter ODE suppliers were also inspected and pinned.

No original Statement/Definition changed, so no public-interface consumer propagation was triggered by the mechanical B-example repair. Current run-manifest inspection found these existing direct consumers of the finite-regulator definition: the local slice theorem, its two B examples, and batch 20's `def-qftx-formal-perturbative-yang-mills-data`, `pthm-qftx-perturbative-yang-mills-st-identity`, and `ex-qftx-finite-orbit-faddeev-popov-jacobian`. No manifest currently claims the absent Singer carrier as a logical supplier. The new proposed supplier consumers are recorded item by item; the final Singer carrier consumes the analytic bundle, irreducible weak-contractibility, reduced-group nontriviality and section/retract results. Batch 20 was not edited.

