100:1.1 The finite center $Z_N$ acts freely on the Banach Lie group $\mathcal G_{k+1}$ by translations. Around any group element choose an open chart whose distinct $Z_N$-translates are disjoint; the quotient map restricts to a chart on each such neighborhood, with transition maps given by smooth translations. Multiplication and inversion descend smoothly, so $\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ is a Banach Lie group. Since a central gauge section acts trivially on connections, this is the structure group of the reduced action. [A1, F1, algebra]
102:1.2 The tangent space of the Coulomb slice at $A$ is $\ker d_A^*$. By [F3], the bounded linear map $L_A:\ker d_A^*\oplus H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)\to H^k(T^*M\otimes\operatorname{ad}P)$, $L_A(a,\xi)=a+d_A\xi$, is an isomorphism with inverse $v\mapsto(v-d_AG_Ad_A^*v,G_Ad_A^*v)$. The derivative of $(a,\xi)\mapsto(A+a)^{\exp\xi}$ at $(0,0)$ is $L_A$ by [F1]. [F1, F3]
104:1.3 Define $F(a,\xi):=d_A^*((A+a)^{\exp\xi}-A)$ for $a\in H^k(T^*M\otimes\operatorname{ad}P)$ near $0$ and $\xi\in H^{k+1}(\operatorname{ad}P)$ near $0$. This is a smooth map into $H^{k-1}(\operatorname{ad}P)$ by [F1], and $F(0,0)=0$. Its partial derivative in $\xi$ at $(0,0)$ is $d_A^*d_A=\Delta_A$, an isomorphism by [F3]. The implicit-function theorem [F4] therefore gives, for every connection sufficiently near $A$, a unique nearby gauge parameter moving it into the Coulomb condition $d_A^*(B^u-A)=0$. This supplies a local section of the orbit projection. [F1, F3, F4]
106:2.1 Apply the inverse-function theorem [F4] to $\Phi_A(s,u):=s^u$ on the local Banach product $S_{A,\varepsilon}\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ near $(A,e)$; its derivative is $L_A$ from step 1.2. Shrink $\varepsilon$ so the slice remains in the open irreducible locus. At $(s,e)$, for $s=A+a$, the derivative is $L_s(b,\xi)=b+d_s\xi$ on $b\in\ker d_A^*$; this differs from $L_A$ only by $(b,\xi)\mapsto[a,\xi]$. Its operator norm is at most $C\|a\|_{H^k}$ by [F7]. Choose $\varepsilon$ so $C\varepsilon\|L_A^{-1}\|<1$; then [F5] makes $L_s$ an isomorphism for every $s$ in the slice. At an arbitrary $(s,u_0)$, write $u=u_0v$ near $u_0$ and use $s^{u_0v}=(s^{u_0})^v$. Applying the inverse action by $u_0$ to the target tangent and changing the gauge tangent by the invertible adjoint action identifies this derivative with $L_s$. Hence the derivative is an isomorphism at every point of the slice product, and the inverse-function theorem makes $\Phi_A$ a local diffeomorphism throughout. [F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F7, step 1.2]
108:3.1 The map $\Phi_A$ is injective after possibly shrinking the slice further. Otherwise there would be sequences $s_j,t_j\in S_{A,1/j}$ and distinct pairs $(s_j,u_j)\ne(t_j,v_j)$ with $s_j^{u_j}=t_j^{v_j}$. Put $w_j=u_jv_j^{-1}$; then $s_j^{w_j}=t_j$ and $s_j,t_j\to A$. Properness [F2] gives a subsequence of $w_j$ converging to $w$ with $A^w=A$. Freeness [F2] gives $w=e$. Hence for all sufficiently large indices, both $(s_j,w_j)$ and $(t_j,e)$ lie in the local injectivity neighborhood from step 2.1. Their equal images force $s_j=t_j$ and $w_j=e$, and freeness then gives $u_j=v_j$, contradicting distinctness. [F2, step 2.1]
110:4.1 Let $U_A:=\Phi_A(S_{A,\varepsilon}\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1})$. It is open because $\Phi_A$ is a local diffeomorphism at every point of its domain, and it is exactly the saturation of the slice. By steps 3.1 and 2.1, $\Phi_A$ is a bijective local diffeomorphism onto $U_A$, hence a diffeomorphism. If $V_A:=\pi_k(S_{A,\varepsilon})$, then $\pi_k^{-1}(V_A)=U_A$. For every open subset $O$ of the slice, $O\times\bar{\mathcal G}_{k+1}$ is open and its image under $\Phi_A$ is open; thus $\pi_k(O)$ is open in $V_A$ by the quotient topology. Therefore the slice-to-quotient map is a smooth chart and $\Phi_A$ is an equivariant local principal trivialization. [step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]
